\section{Contributions}
\label{sec:contributions}

This thesis delivers the following contributions to the problem domain:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The artefact RP}: an algorithm-assisted planning platform built on Next.js, tRPC, and PostgreSQL, with three optimisation engines (a greedy heuristic, OR-Tools CP-SAT, and Timefold) and a Gantt-style drag-and-drop human-in-the-loop timeline.
  \item \textbf{An interview corpus} with traffic coordinators in Norwegian transport companies, conducted alongside an ongoing dialogue with Admmit, surfacing the visibility gap and the operator-vs-owner asymmetry that anchor the discussion.
  \item \textbf{A multi-engine benchmark} comparing the three solvers on identical synthetic datasets that span the fleet ranges of the interviewed companies, under a shared time budget and a shared lex-tier scorer.
  \item \textbf{A requirements traceability matrix} recording implementation and developer-verified behaviour for thirty-seven in-scope functional requirements (FK-01 through FK-37).
  \item \textbf{An eight-iteration narrative} of the artefact's construction, each iteration carrying an inline origin label (Admmit-mandate, interview-validated, designer-technical, or a mixture) so a reader can trace any design decision back to its origin.
  \item \textbf{A bounded discussion} that organises the primary findings under the three locked anchor concepts (\textbf{Efficiency}, \textbf{Trust/control}, \textbf{Adaptability}) and names twelve hierarchical limitations (L1 through L12) that bound what the thesis can and cannot claim.
\end{itemize}


\section{Scope and Delimitations}
\label{sec:scope}

\section{Thesis Structure}
\label{sec:thesis-structure}

The thesis is organised as follows:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{\Cref{chap:theory}} establishes the theoretical and disciplinary background: resource scheduling, human-in-the-loop automation (Bainbridge, Hoff and Bashir, Miller, Lee and See), Transport Management Systems, and Design Science Research.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{chap:method}} sets out the Design Science Research method as applied through Peffers' six DSRM activities, the eight-iteration construction of RP, and the evaluation framework.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{chap:findings}} reports what the interviews surfaced, what the artefact delivers against its functional requirements, and how the three optimisation engines compare on the multi-engine benchmark.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{chap:discussion}} interprets the findings under the three anchor concepts, works through adoption and sustainability, and names the twelve hierarchical limitations (L1 through L12) that bound what the thesis can claim.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{chap:conclusion}} answers each sub-question against the body of evidence and outlines future work.
\end{itemize}


\section{Approach}
\label{sec:approach}

\Cref{chap:method} describes the method and the iterations in full.


\section{Additional Resources}
\label{sec:additional-resources}

The artefact source code, the synthetic benchmark datasets, and the requirements traceability matrix referenced throughout this thesis are tracked in the project's Git repository alongside the thesis source:

\begin{itemize}
  \item RP lives in \texttt{ressursplanlegger/} as a Git submodule of the thesis repository.
  \item The benchmark protocol described in \Cref{sec:eval-benchmark} is implemented in \texttt{ressursplanlegger/src/optimization/benchmark.ts}, with the three synthetic datasets stored under \texttt{ressursplanlegger/datasets/}.
  \item The requirements traceability matrix referenced in \Cref{sec:eval-traceability} is maintained alongside the codebase and reproduced in summary form in \Cref{chap:findings}.
\end{itemize}
